STAGED PATCH 0005 (kernel round) of 5: apply after 0001.
Targets: Linux 6.12.116, mm/percpu.c, lib/genalloc.c,
mm/slub.c. Line anchors are the manifest's numbers of
record, checked against the pristine tree; context
lines are schematic. Re-base on the real tree before
applying.

Subject: iscandar: the second ring in kernel form
(4.2, 4.3), and the alloc_bulk funnel

Center 4.1 (mempool) takes no hunk, as in both prior
rounds: a mempool over slab is redirected at 1.1 with
its elements, and a mempool over pages draws on the
substrate, exactly as the reconciliation disposes the
family.

Center 4.2 (percpu). The door stands at the center,
as ruled and as verified in the tree at the bench
(2026-11-20: the alloc door at the head of
pcpu_alloc_noprof, the free door above the chunk
search in free_percpu). Two changes in kernel form.
First, the bridge door's atomic-context refusal is
removed: it existed because the bridge call sleeps,
and the direct call does not (design section 3), so
the refusal has no remaining reason. The mirrored
rejection set stays, word for word: it is the stock
contract, not a serving condition. Second, the
zeroing covers the whole run; under the build's
uniprocessor precondition (num_possible_cpus() is 1)
that is byte-for-byte the bridge door's memset of
size, and the comment says so, so the shape survives
any later reading of the door out of context. The
chunk bookkeeping stays the kernel's over the run, as
the reconciliation states; the center's earliest
traffic falls in the prefix and is stock and counted
there, not missed.

Center 4.3 (gen_pool). The bridge gated on pool
registration; the audit replaced registration with
the backing rule (item 3, ruled 2026-11-19), and this
patch implements the rule where the audit puts it:
the gate's test runs on each chunk as it is added.
Every add path in this tree funnels through
gen_pool_add_owner (gen_pool_add and gen_pool_add_virt
are header inlines ending there), so one hook at its
successful end sees every chunk, with the chunk's
physical address in hand for the core's RAM test. The
alloc door consults the recorded decision; the free
door decides by range alone, as every free door in
this design does, so a grant made while a pool stood
served still frees home if the pool is later marked
stock (the core prints that flip as a finding at the
moment it happens, 0001).

The alloc_bulk funnel (mm/slub.c), the audit's item 2
as ruled: funnel it. The full-SLUB body of
__kmem_cache_alloc_bulk (the live body: CONFIG_SLUB=y,
SLUB_TINY not set, read from the build's .config at
the bench) drains the per-CPU freelist privately,
under the cpu_slab lock, with a tid bump and a
slowpath fallback when the list runs dry. The funnel
replaces the whole drain with a loop over the door'd
center, slab_alloc_node, one call per object. Three
consequences, each intended:
- kfence needs no line of the funnel's own: the
  center performs it, in the center's own order
  (hook, kfence, fastpath), which is the order the
  stock drain kept by hand.
- The lock, the tid bookkeeping, and the slowpath
  fallback disappear with the drain: the center
  carries its own synchronization, and when the door
  is active the core's irqsave section is the
  synchronization. During the prefix the center
  serves stock, so a prefix bulk call is stock-served
  whole, counted at 1.1.
- The error path changes its free: it named the
  static __kmem_cache_free_bulk, which is correct for
  stock objects and wrong for arena ones, since it
  never passes the 1.4 door. A funneled fill can hold
  arena objects, so the error path frees through
  kmem_cache_free_bulk, the door'd entry, whose range
  decision sends each pointer home by its own birth.
  This is the funnel's twin: miss it, and the first
  failed bulk fill leaks arena objects into the stock
  freelist, which is precisely the failure class the
  coverage audit exists to prevent. (The SLUB_TINY
  body's error path has the identical line; it is
  not compiled here, and its body already funnels by
  construction, but if TINY is ever enabled the same
  one-word change is owed there, and this note is
  where that debt is recorded.)
The all-or-nothing contract is preserved exactly: any
failure frees the partial fill and returns 0.

--- a/mm/percpu.c
+++ b/mm/percpu.c
@@ -1743,7 +1743,30 @@
-void __percpu *pcpu_alloc_noprof(size_t size, size_t align, bool reserved, ...)
+void __percpu *pcpu_alloc_noprof(size_t size, size_t align, bool reserved, ...)
 {
+	if (iscandar_active() && !reserved) {
+		void *p;
+
+		/* Center 4.2 is served under the build's
+		 * uniprocessor precondition: on a UP build the
+		 * percpu translations are the identity, so the
+		 * arena address is the token, whole and exact,
+		 * and the run's size is size times a possible
+		 * CPU count of 1. The checks mirror the stock
+		 * rejection set below, which this door sits
+		 * above. Unlike the bridge door, an
+		 * atomic-context caller is NOT refused: the
+		 * direct call does not sleep (design section
+		 * 3), so the refusal has no remaining reason.
+		 * The run is zeroed here, as the chunk layer's
+		 * population would have done. Ruled
+		 * 2026-11-17; the scope is stated in the
+		 * paper. */
+		if (unlikely(!size || size > PCPU_MIN_UNIT_SIZE
+			     || align > PAGE_SIZE
+			     || !is_power_of_2(align)))
+			return NULL;
+		p = iscandar_alloc((u64)size * num_possible_cpus(),
+				   (unsigned int)align, 0);
+		if (!p)
+			return NULL;
+		memset(p, 0, (u64)size * num_possible_cpus());
+		return (void __percpu *)p;
+	}
+	iscandar_prefix_note(ISCANDAR_CENTER_PERCPU, size);
 	...
 }
@@ -2234,6 +2257,18 @@
-void free_percpu(void __percpu *ptr)
+void free_percpu(void __percpu *ptr)
 {
 	...
+	/* Center 4.2's free door, above the chunk search:
+	 * on a UP build the token is the arena address
+	 * itself, so the range test is exact. The chunk
+	 * search below has no chunk for an arena-born
+	 * token and must never see one. (The door sits
+	 * after the NULL check and the kmemleak hook, as
+	 * the bridge round placed it; verified in the
+	 * tree at the bench, 2026-11-20.) */
+	if (iscandar_ptr_in_arena(ptr)) {
+		iscandar_free((const void *)ptr, 0);
+		return;
+	}
 	...
 }
